    {
      "id": "GENUS_PSEUDOPARAMACRODEROIDES",
      "name": "Pseudoparamacroderoides",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Pseudoparamacroderoides — a genus of trematodas.",
      "lineage": "Pseudoparamacroderoides",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "SP_PSEUDOPARAMACRODEROIDES_DONGTHAPENSIS_TRUONG_CURRAN_BULLARD_2021",
          "name": "Pseudoparamacroderoides dongthapensis Truong, Curran & Bullard, 2021",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Pseudoparamacroderoides",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "(Fig. 24) Record. 1. Truong et al. (2021). Truong et al. (2021) established P. dongthapensis for specimens infecting the intestine of the whiskered bagrid catfish, Mystus mysticetus Roberts (Siluriformes: Bagridae), from the Mekong River at Cao Lanh Fish Market, Dong Thap Province, Vietnam. This species was characterized from P. seenghali (and its synonym, P. vittati) by the following features: i) a more elongate hindbody, more than twice the length of the forebody (2.2 – 2.6 ×) vs less than twice the length of the forebody in P. seenghali (1.4 – 1.9 ×); ii) a longer excretory vesicle extending anteriad beyond the anterior testis (49 – 55 % of body length) vs a shorter one reaching the mid-level of the posterior or anterior testis (32 – 44 % of body length) in P. seenghali; iii) a uterus extending posteriorly into the post-cecal space near the posterior extremity of the body (very small post-uterine space ~ 1 % of body length) vs P. seenghali which has a uterus restricted to the cecal space (post-uterine space ~ 10 – 13 % of body length); iv) a shorter esophagus (6 – 12 % of body length) vs a longer one in P. vittati (= P. seenghali) (19 % of body length); v) a cirrus-pouch extending posteriad to the ventral sucker vs restricted to the area around the ventral sucker, not surpassing its posterior margin in the original description of P. seenghali; and vi) proportionally larger testes (diameter> 1 / 4 of maximum body width) vs a smaller value for this feature in P.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:11438998"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_PSEUDOPARAMACRODEROIDES_KENI_AGARWAL_AGARWAL_1985",
          "name": "Pseudoparamacroderoides keni Agarwal & Agarwal, 1985",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Pseudoparamacroderoides",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "",
          "sourcedFrom": "none"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_PSEUDOPARAMACRODEROIDES_PSEUDOBAGRI_KARAR_BLEND_DRONAN_ADEL_2023",
          "name": "Pseudoparamacroderoides pseudobagri  Karar, Blend, Dronan & Adel, 2023",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Pseudoparamacroderoides",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "(Fig. 25) (Syns. Astiotrema pseudobagri [Wang in Wang, Zhao, Chen & Tao, 1983] Karar, Blend, Dronen & Adel, 2021 n. syn.; Gauhatiana pseudobagri Wang in Wang, Zhao, Chen & Tao, 1983 n. syn.) Record. 1. Wang et al. (1983). As previously mentioned, Karar et al. (2021) re-assigned G. pseudobagri into Astiotrema as A. pseudobagri based on its lack of one of the key characteristics of Gauhatiana, vitellarium divided into two separate clusters on each side of the body. Furthermore, Karar et al. (2021) considered A. pseudobagri a junior subjective synonym of A. reniferum. Karar et al. (2021) did not specify the presence of an I-shaped excretory vesicle in the specimens of Wang et al. (1983) illustrated in the original description (see Wang et al. 1983, fig. 4). As a result of the problematic state of the specimens of Kumari et al. (1972) (discussed earlier), in particular, the variation of the excretory vesicle shape from I-shaped to Y-shaped with intermediate phases, Karar et al. (2021) did not emphasize this feature as one of the variables subject to intraspecific variation. Furthermore, species of Astiotrema (sensu stricto) have been recorded within the intestine of a wide variety of host groups including some freshwater fishes. However, no freshwater bagrid catfish has been reported as a host for any taxa of Astiotrema except for the disputed reports of Kumari et al. (1972) and Wang et al. (1983). The earlier specimens of A. reniferum of Kumari et al.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:12256023"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_PSEUDOPARAMACRODEROIDES_RAYCHAUDHURII_AGARWAL_KUMAR_1984",
